Maureen Daly: "Lewis, it's Maureen again — I've been thinking on what you said about the queues at the Food Hall. 152 people walking out the door is a fair chunk of trade. What's actually going wrong — tills, staffing, or the way the baskets work? I'm not fishing for a favour, I just reckon there might be something I could do for you, and honestly the extra would help me no end."
Lewis Pryce: "Maureen, I appreciate it, I do. Honestly it's the tills and the staffing both, but mostly it's that Owen's on 38 hours and we're one body short when the Friday rush hits. Two people can serve about 340 a week and we're turning away 152 on top of that. The baskets are fine, it's just not enough hands. What were you thinking you could do?"
Maureen Daly: "Well, I was thinking I could pick up a few shifts — I'm already on 38 hours at the Corner Stores, but I could do a Friday evening or a Saturday morning, whatever suits. I know the tills and the baskets inside out from Dave's place, so I'd not need much showing. Would that help you out — and would there be a bit of pay in it for me?"
Lewis Pryce: "Right, Maureen, I'll be straight with you. Friday evenings are the worst of it, so a Friday evening shift would actually help. I can't pay you much while the shop's tight, but I'll put an offer on the table and you can tell me if it's worth your while. Let me check the numbers first."
Maureen Daly: "That's fair enough, Lewis — you check your numbers and I'll be honest with you when I hear them. I'm not after much, just something that makes the Friday rush easier on you and a few pound in my pocket. When would you know? I'm on at Dave's till Saturday but I could talk any evening."

