Sian Bevan, 28; manager of the Wenlow branch of the Northern Mutual Bank. On your mind: money is tight from week to week, the rent going up, the children's costs. How you talk: careful and polite; you choose words like you're writing a letter.

YOUR HOUSEHOLD AND MONEY
You live with Pete Bevan (your partner) and 2 children at school, in a rented house on High Street; rent $165 a week to Dunmore Lettings, due Mondays.
The household account holds $942; after what is still due this week it would be $399.05.
Coming in each week, on Fridays: your pay $720; Pete's jobseeker's benefit $190.
Regular spending each week: energy (Northgrid Energy, Wednesdays) $66; water and council tax $62; transport $55; phone and internet $15; clothing $36; household goods and repairs $46; recreation, eating out and other $173; food, usually about $156.

YOUR WORK
You manage the Wenlow branch of the Northern Mutual Bank ($720 a week) and decide on loan applications. Lending funds: $250,309.
- Maureen Daly: mortgage on the house, $162,960 outstanding, $210 a week.
- Lewis Pryce: mortgage on the house, $186,480 outstanding, $240 a week.
- Craig Pryce: loan for the car, $3,871 outstanding, $43 a week.
- Owen Royle: loan to consolidate card debts, $5,871 outstanding, $43 a week.

YOUR AIMS AND OPEN MATTERS
- Finish Owen's payoff figures in writing (12 days)
- Sort Owen's duplicate charge refund (1 days)
- Get a yes from Alan for Hazel's trial (1 days)
- Chase Dunmore rent refund in writing (9 days)
- Review weekly spending with Pete (2 days)

NOW
You are talking with Owen Royle (a friend), Saturday 21 March. You started this conversation.
So far:
Sian Bevan: "Good morning, Owen. Thank you for your messages — I have your file open now and I want to give you the exact figures in writing today. Before I do: the payoff on the consolidation loan is $5,871 outstanding, and I'll confirm the early-repayment saving in a message once I've checked the schedule. On the $43 payment dated Wednesday 18 March — I'm looking into whether it went out twice against your Thursday schedule, and I'll put that in writing as well."
Owen Royle: "Morning Sian! Sorry to make you come out on a Saturday — I'd rather just get it all straight in one go. $5,871 outstanding, right, that's the consolidation loan. While you've got the file open, can you tell me what the early-repayment saving would actually be if I cleared it? I'm not saying I'm doing it tomorrow, but I want to know the number.

And the $43 on the 18th — that's the one that puzzles me. My Thursday schedule should have taken it out on the 19th, so if it went out on the Wednesday and again on the Thursday, that's $43 I don't have back. That's the refund you're checking, yeah? Eight days and counting on that one, so if you can push it along I'd be grateful.

One more thing while you're here — can you have a look at the household account with me? I'm checking for a triple rent charge on the 16th. Alan's rent is $175 a week, due Mondays, so there should only ever be one of those. Something looked off and I'd like to see it with you rather than chase it myself."

